Compilation: 

unpack lvg.tar.gz (tar xvzf lvg.tar.gz)
make

that should compile.  If you have expectk installed on your system,
you can use the graphical interface (lvg_gui, you will have to modify
the path in the first line of this text file).  If not, you have to
use the manual lvg program.

Before using it, define an environmental variable, LVG_Data, to point
to the directory where the data are, i.e.

(t)csh:  setenv LVG_Data path_to_Data_directory
bash:    export LVG_Data=path_to_Data_directory

then run ./lvg

This can be run as single run or as loop.  The input parameters mean:

Molecule: pick one from list 

Number of Levels: as many as you want - there is a safeguard, so it
	  does not use more levels than are available, but if you use
	  many more than you need (e.g. no highly excited levels if
	  you want to run dark cloud models), this can bomb the
	  program, and in the best case will just make it run longer

Init: use whatever, it shouldn't matter

Mode: single or loop, depending on what you want.  For loop, one
      defines ranges and either steps (for Temperature) or factors
      (for density, colum density etc.)

T_kin:	   kinetic temperature
n(H2):	    density
X(e):	    electron density - only relevant for HCO+
N(mol):	    molecular column density
T_dust:	    temperature of internal dust
N(H_2):	    H_2 column density, only used for internal dust radiation
delta_v:    line width - remember that in the LVG approximation only 
	    N(mol)/delta_v (= X(mol)/(dv/dr) is relevant)
Tback:	    temperature of background grey body
beta_back:  dust opacity index of background grey body
tau100mic:  opacity at 100 mic of background grey body
Wback:	    geometric filling factor of background grey body
Te:	    electron temperature of background HII region
EM:	    emission measure of background HII region
WHII:	    geometric filling factor of background HII region
T_cosmic:   cosmic background temperature, 2.73 K for our Galaxy


Output:

There are three outputs: one on the screen, one in <molecule>.out
(copy of screen output, for human consumption) and one in
<molecule.plt> (for computer consumption).  The parameters on the
screen mean:

Ji, Jf, Ki, Kf etc.:	quantum numbers
T_ex:			excitation temperature
freq:			frequency of transition
tau(Back):		opacity of background grey body
T_l(Back):		brightness temperature of background grey body
tau(HII):		opacity of background HII region
T_l(HII):		brightness temperature of background HII
			region
T_bg:			T_l(Back)+T_l(HII)
tau:			opacity of molecular line
T_l:			line temperature - in front of background
			sources, so absorption is possible


In <molecule>.plt, the same information can be found, but to read the
stuff e.g. into GreG, one has to compare with <molecule>.out and count
the columns - it's a bit awkward, but the exact information in there
is different for different molecules.

In case of trouble, please contact me: schilke@mpifr-bonn.mpg.de

I don't give any warranty, use at your own risk.  It's a human
product, and may contain bugs.

If you use this program in any paper, please mention it in the
acknowledgements.

Enjoy!

	Peter

